# popo — Cline Memory Bank Rules

I am an expert software engineer building popo, a Windows system-level voice dictation tool.
My memory resets completely between sessions. I MUST read ALL memory-bank/ files at the start
of EVERY task before doing anything. This is non-negotiable.

## On Every Session Start:
1. Read memory-bank/projectbrief.md
2. Read memory-bank/productContext.md
3. Read memory-bank/systemPatterns.md
4. Read memory-bank/techContext.md
5. Read memory-bank/activeContext.md
6. Read memory-bank/progress.md
7. Only then say: "Context loaded. Continuing from: [last active task from activeContext.md]"

## Memory Bank Update Triggers:
- After completing any phase milestone
- Before context window fills (when responses slow down)
- When user says "update memory bank"
- After any architectural decision is made

## When "update memory bank" is said:
Review and update ALL six files even if some seem unchanged.
Capture: what was built, what decisions were made, what the next step is.

## Plan Mode:
Use for strategy, architecture decisions, design review.
Never start implementing in Plan Mode.

## Act Mode:
Use for writing code, creating files, running commands.
Always start Act Mode by confirming the current task from activeContext.md.

## Learned Patterns (append discoveries here as work progresses):
- POPO_BRIEF.md (project root) is the single source of truth — every design/tech
  question resolves to the brief first; deviation requires an explicit note here.
- Phase 1 deliverables live in docs/: COMPETITIVE_ANALYSIS.md, TECHNICAL_SPEC.md,
  DESIGN_SYSTEM.md, PASTE_MECHANICS.md. They were produced before any code.
- The pill is ONE morphing motion.div — never component swaps. State machine lives
  in Zustand pillStore; Rust emits "pill:state:*" events; React only listens.
- Paste flow is strictly ordered: GetForegroundWindow → save clipboard →
  write text → SetForegroundWindow → sleep 50ms → enigo Ctrl+V → sleep 100ms →
  restore clipboard. Any fallback (WM_PASTE / UIAutomation / AttachThreadInput)
  must preserve the same ordering.
- Pill window NEVER receives focus: WS_EX_NOACTIVATE + WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW applied in
  Tauri setup hook via windows crate. setIgnoreCursorEvents toggled per state.

### Session 2 (scaffold) discoveries

- **SKILLS GAP — UNRESOLVED**: The brief (Section 1) requires reading four skill
  files (`impeccable`, `minimalist-ui`, `industrial-brutalist-ui`,
  `design-taste-frontend`) from the "project skills directory" BEFORE writing any
  UI component. No such directory exists in this project. Phase 1 produced only
  docs so this did not block, but PHASE 3 ITEM [8] (the first real UI component —
  PillOverlay) MUST NOT BEGIN until either (a) those skill files are provided to
  the project, or (b) the user explicitly waives the requirement. Ask the user
  at the start of Phase 3.
- **Brief typo — fix applied**: The brief's Cargo.toml snippet lists
  `gcp-auth = "0.12"`. The real crate on crates.io is named `gcp_auth` (underscore).
  The scaffold uses the correct underscore form. No other brief deviations so far.
- **Tauri v2 pill HWND hook**: Confirmed `windows::Win32::UI::WindowsAndMessaging::{GetWindowLongPtrW, SetWindowLongPtrW, WS_EX_NOACTIVATE, WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW}`
  is the correct path in `windows` 0.58. Applied in `lib.rs` setup hook after
  `app.get_webview_window("pill")`.
- **Icons are deferred**: Tauri requires icon files at `src-tauri/icons/`.
  Phase 2 scaffold ships only a README in that directory; the first run of
  `pnpm tauri dev` or `pnpm tauri build` will need at least `32x32.png` and
  `icon.ico`. Generate via `pnpm tauri icon <source.png>` once the mark SVG exists.
- **Shared types flow**: Canonical definitions live in `packages/shared-types/src/index.ts`.
  `apps/desktop/src/types/*.ts` re-export from there. Don't duplicate types.
- **Vite alias + TS paths**: both `tsconfig.json` and `vite.config.ts` map
  `@popo/shared-types` to the source file. Keep them in sync when the package
  root changes.

### Session 3 (skills integration) discoveries

- **SKILLS GAP RESOLVED**. User supplied two folders at project root:
  - `impeccable-style-universal/.claude/skills/impeccable/` — the `impeccable`
    skill (universal frontend quality; SKILL.md + 30+ reference/*.md files).
    Register for popo: **product** (not brand).
  - `taste-skill-main/taste-skill-main/skills/{taste-skill, minimalist-skill,
    brutalist-skill, ...}/SKILL.md` — the `design-taste-frontend`,
    `minimalist-ui`, and `industrial-brutalist-ui` skills.
- **All four skills have been read and reconciled**. Integration lives in
  `docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md` §12 ("Skill application matrix"). The Section 10
  design-lint checklist was extended with skill-derived lints. Before any UI
  component, the agent must read §12 alongside the brief.
- **Metric dials fixed at 4 / 4 / 4** (DESIGN_VARIANCE / MOTION_INTENSITY /
  VISUAL_DENSITY). Raising any dial requires updating DESIGN_SYSTEM.md §12.2
  + §12.8 with rationale. No ad-hoc escalation.
- **Four brief-vs-skill conflicts resolved in favor of the brief**, each
  logged in §12:
  1. History active-row 2px left edge (impeccable bans >1px side stripes).
  2. ModeEditor as modal (impeccable bans modal-first thinking).
  3. Pill's `rounded-full` shape (minimalist-ui bans `rounded-full` on large
     containers).
  4. Dark canvas `#080808` (minimalist-ui prescribes light bone `#F7F6F3`).
- **brutalist-skill is mostly REJECTED** — popo is not Swiss-print / CRT
  terminal. Only narrow borrowings kept: monospace for data (already in brief),
  1px row separators in Settings (already in brief), `<kbd>` keystroke chips
  (already in brief). Rejected: `border-radius: 0`, ASCII framing, CRT scanlines,
  halftone filters, Aviation red `#E61919`, `clamp(4rem, 10vw, 15rem)` display.
- **Phase 3 item [8] is now UNBLOCKED**. First real UI component (PillOverlay)
  may be written when the user says go. At the start of that work, re-read:
  (a) POPO_BRIEF.md §2, (b) DESIGN_SYSTEM.md §7 + §10 + §12,
  (c) impeccable `reference/product.md` for register-specific rules,
  (d) impeccable `reference/motion-design.md` for the spring/ease specifics.
- **OKLCH deviation logged**: impeccable prefers OKLCH, popo's `globals.css`
  uses hex for brief fidelity. When we do the color-tuning pass (Phase 3 polish
  or Phase 5 harden), convert tokens to OKLCH and reduce chroma at L<10% and
  L>95%.
- **Type scale ratio slightly below impeccable's ≥1.25**: popo ratios sit at
  1.15–1.22. Kept per brief; acceptable because Geist Pixel variants provide
  weight/width differentiation. Re-evaluate in Phase 3 PillOverlay work.

### Session 4 (PillOverlay + scaffold verification) discoveries

- **Phase 3 item [8] COMPLETE**: the PillOverlay component (single
  morphing `motion.div`) is implemented in
  `apps/desktop/src/components/pill/PillOverlay.tsx`. Sibling components
  (WaveformCanvas, WaveformContent, ProcessingContent, SuccessContent,
  ErrorContent, DotMatrix placeholder) and hooks (usePillEvents for Rust
  event subscription, usePillDemo for dev-only state cycling) are all
  installed. Set-ignore-cursor-events toggled per state in PillPage.
  Reduced-motion handled via Framer Motion's `useReducedMotion`.
- **`geist` npm package is Next.js-only.** It imports `next/font/local`
  which does not exist in Vite. We work around by running
  `scripts/copy-geist-fonts.mjs` (predev + prebuild hooks) which copies
  the five `.woff2` files from `node_modules/.pnpm/geist@.../dist/fonts/
  geist-pixel/` to `apps/desktop/public/fonts/`. `fonts.css` declares
  `@font-face` rules pointing at `/fonts/GeistPixel-*.woff2`. DESIGN_SYSTEM
  §2 is not updated (the variant assignments still hold); only the
  install mechanism changed. The brief's `import { GeistPixelSquare }
  from 'geist/font/pixel'` snippet is a Next.js example and does not apply
  to popo's Vite stack.
- **Icons: placeholder script committed.** `scripts/gen-placeholder-icons.mjs`
  emits minimal 1×1 dark PNGs + a 16×16 dark ICO so `cargo check` and
  `pnpm tauri dev` don't error on missing icons. Real icons pending via
  `pnpm tauri icon <logo.png>` when the mark SVG exists. Also: **`icon.icns`
  removed** from `tauri.conf.json` `bundle.icon` because popo is Windows-only
  and Tauri's bundler validates every listed file's existence at build.
- **Tauri v2 + `generate_context!()` validates `frontendDist` at Rust compile
  time**, even though `tauri dev` uses `devUrl` at runtime. Created
  `apps/desktop/dist/.gitkeep` so the directory exists; real build output
  overwrites.
- **tsconfig project-references were removed.** The initial
  `tsconfig.node.json` was creating a `tsc -b` build that required each
  referenced project to emit, which conflicted with our `noEmit: true`.
  Simpler: `tsc --noEmit` directly on `tsconfig.json`. `tsconfig.node.json`
  was deleted.
- **Vite config trailing commas**: `vite.config.ts` used `async () =>`
  which isn't a valid overload for `defineConfig` in Vite 5.4+. Switched
  to synchronous `() =>`. Also removed the `minify` branch to avoid
  `string | boolean` widening; we just use Vite's `esbuild` default.
- **Zero-error compile state verified** at end of session:
  - `pnpm --filter desktop typecheck` → clean
  - `pnpm --filter desktop build` (`tsc --noEmit && vite build`) → clean
    (4960 modules, 293KB → 96KB gz)
  - `cargo check` in `src-tauri/` → clean in 6.94s
- **`pnpm tauri dev` has NOT been run this session.** The ultimate smoke
  test (does the transparent pill actually show on screen with
  WS_EX_NOACTIVATE behavior intact?) is the user's first action in the
  next session. Expected result: a near-invisible 48×6 line at bottom
  center. With `?demo=1` on the pill URL (set via tauri.conf.json or a
  temporary env var), the pill cycles through all six states.
- **pnpm-lock.yaml is gitignored elsewhere but not explicitly in `.gitignore`** —
  review in a later tidy pass. pnpm writes one automatically.
- **Known runtime risk**: `setIgnoreCursorEvents` on the pill window calls
  `@tauri-apps/api/webviewWindow`'s `getCurrentWebviewWindow().setIgnoreCursorEvents`.
  Wrapped in try/catch so non-Tauri contexts (pure Vite) don't crash. Verified
  in Vite build output; runtime verification pending tauri dev.

### Session 5 (first tauri dev — runtime fixes)

- **BLACK-BOX BUG FIXED.** First `pnpm tauri dev` showed a fully opaque
  dark 340×80 rectangle instead of a near-invisible sleep pill. Root
  cause: `globals.css` set `html, body, #root { background: var(--bg-base) }`
  globally, and the `:has([data-pill-surface])` override only targeted
  `body` (not html/root). Fixed by flipping the default: html/body/#root
  default to `background: transparent`, and the main window opts in via
  `html[data-window="main"]` selector which `main.tsx` sets synchronously
  before React mounts (based on `window.location.pathname !== "/pill"`).
  No flash, no race.
- **Tray icon wired in `lib.rs`.** Removed the `trayIcon` config from
  `tauri.conf.json` and built the tray in Rust's setup hook via
  `tauri::tray::TrayIconBuilder`. Tray has tooltip "popo", Open/Quit
  menu, and left-click opens + focuses the main window. Prior to this,
  `tauri dev` had no visible UI affordance because the main window
  starts `visible: false` and the pill is `WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW` (invisible
  in taskbar/Alt-Tab). Tray icon is now the only visible popo surface
  at idle — exactly per brief.
- **Real icons generated from user-provided SVG.** Script
  `scripts/gen-icons-from-svg.mjs` uses sharp (added as root devDep) to
  rasterize the canonical waveform glyph at 16/32/48/256 and pack a
  multi-res .ico. Also emits 32x32.png, 128x128.png, 128x128@2x.png
  (256×256), icon.png (64×64 tray). All in `--text-primary` (#EDEBE6)
  on transparent. Icon source is embedded in the script — edit there
  and re-run to update. The prior placeholder script
  (`gen-placeholder-icons.mjs`) is kept for fallback CI scenarios.
- **Icon design decision**: the user's source SVG was 600×400 with black
  on white plus a gooey-filter blob effect. Reframed to 600×600 square
  and inverted to warm-white on transparent. Windows taskbar is dark
  on most modern setups; warm-white reads crisply. Gooey filter kept
  (librsvg/sharp supports `feGaussianBlur` + `feColorMatrix` + `feComposite`).
- **User-facing dev note**: in sleep state the pill is literally
  48×6 at opacity 0.18, which looks like nothing on a dark desktop.
  To visually verify the component, the user should either:
  (a) set `VITE_PILL_DEMO=1` in `apps/desktop/.env.local`, OR
  (b) temporarily append `?demo=1` to the pill URL in tauri.conf.json.
  Both trigger `usePillDemo` which cycles all 6 states with synthetic
  waveform bars.

### Session 6 (Phase 4 [10] AppShell + Sidebar)

- **Deliberate execution-order deviation**. User said "do as you wish",
  picked Option B (UI-first) instead of strict brief [6]→[7]→[9]→[10].
  Rationale: [10] has zero Rust dependency; visible progress matters
  more than strict sequencing; brief's order is risk-first not
  dependency-first; user was morale-low seeing invisible pill. Revert
  to strict order after [10] by doing Phase 2 [6] next.
- **AppShell architecture**: nested routing via react-router `<Route
  element={<AppShell />}>` wrapping all non-pill routes. AppShell owns
  AnimatePresence + motion.div keyed on `location.pathname` so page
  transitions fire on nav click. Pill route stays standalone (never
  wraps).
- **Drag region pattern**: top 40px of both sidebar and content carry
  `data-tauri-drag-region`. WindowControls in content's top-right use
  `data-tauri-drag-region="false"` on each button to stay clickable
  inside the drag zone. Tested in vite build; runtime verification
  pending.
- **Close button hides, not exits**. `handleHide()` calls
  `getCurrentWebviewWindow().hide()`. The tray icon is the only exit
  affordance — right-click tray → Quit. Matches daemon semantics. If
  user complains later we can add `beforeunload` handler or make close
  a true exit.
- **Phosphor icons are imported named** (`Microphone`, `Sliders`, etc.),
  weight `regular` per DESIGN_SYSTEM §3. Sidebar nav at 18px, window
  controls at 12px, pill state icons at 12px.
- **Tooltips are in-flow (not portaled)** — fine for sidebar (parent
  has no `overflow:hidden`). If Phase 4 [11]–[14] pages introduce
  overflow-clipping parents, switch Tooltip to `createPortal`.
- **Sidebar inherits drag-region to children**, so clicking around the
  PopoMark glyph drags the window. Intentional per brief §3 (40px top
  is drag). NavItems below the top 40px are in a separate container,
  no drag inheritance.
- **New page: AccountPage**. The brief lists Account as a sidebar item
  (User icon, pinned bottom) but the Phase 2 scaffold never added it.
  Created as a stub in Session 6.
- **Main-window default route** is `/history` via `<Route index
  element={<Navigate to="/history" replace />} />`. Matches brief §3
  History-first assumption.
- **Window minimize capability** added: `core:window:allow-minimize`
  was missing from `capabilities/default.json`. Tauri denies the API
  call without it — silently caught in our try/catch, so no crash.
- **Build size grew** from 293 KB / 96 KB gz (Session 5) to 330 KB /
  106 KB gz (Session 6). Diff is framer-motion's AnimatePresence chunks
  + react-router NavLink + the new layout components. Still well under
  any budget.
- **Stale rust-analyzer diagnostic**: "OUT_DIR env var is not set" on
  lib.rs line 79 (the `tauri::generate_context!()` call). rust-analyzer
  can't expand the proc-macro without the build script output. `cargo
  check` runs the build script and passes in 18s. Safe to ignore until
  editor restart.
- **Next session priority options** for user to pick:
  1. **Phase 4 [11] History** — real SessionRow UI with synthetic data
     (no Rust needed yet).
  2. **Phase 4 [12] Settings** — real SettingRow primitives.
  3. **Phase 2 [6] Rust core** — return to brief order; backend work
     (cpal + GCP + Win32 focus).
  4. **Phase 4 [13] Modes** — grid + modal editor.
  Recommended: [11] first (momentum from [10], same frontend-only
  territory, unblocks future pill→history flow).

