### Session 12 (Phase 2 [6] Rust core)

- **User said "continue"** after Session 11 — returned to brief order
  with Phase 2 [6] Rust core work. First Rust code in popo that does
  product-level work.
- **Scope for this session** (realistic single-session slice):
  - Real cpal audio capture.
  - 16-bar RMS waveform extraction + 25 Hz event emission.
  - Real Win32 `GetForegroundWindow` + clipboard save/restore helpers.
  - Real global hotkey Ctrl+Shift+Space via
    `tauri-plugin-global-shortcut` with hold/release edge detection.
  - State machine that goes press → record → release → transcribe →
    clipboard → pill success → sleep.
  - **FAKE `gcp::fake_transcribe`** that returns a canned string after
    800 ms. Real gRPC + Chirp streaming deferred to Phase 2 [6] polish
    (needs proto vendoring + protoc + TLS + gcp_auth wiring).
  - **NO actual paste yet** — transcript sits on the clipboard and the
    user Ctrl+Vs themselves. The 4-method paste chain is Phase 2 [7].
- **Files added**:
  - `src/audio/capture.rs` — cpal input stream, f32/i16/u16 format
    handling, bounded VecDeque ring buffer (48_000 samples cap).
    `SafeStream` wrapper with `unsafe impl Send + Sync` for
    `cpal::Stream` (see gotcha below).
  - `src/audio/processor.rs` — `compute_bars()` returns `[f32; 16]`
    from the last 40 ms of audio using plain RMS (no FFT).
  - `src/audio/mod.rs` — public exports.
  - `src/focus/windows.rs` — `get_foreground_hwnd` /
    `set_foreground_hwnd` / `get_clipboard_text` / `set_clipboard_text`.
    HWND stored as `isize` (Send) with conversion at call sites.
  - `src/focus/mod.rs` — platform-gated re-exports; non-Windows stubs
    so `cargo check` on Linux/Mac still compiles.
  - `src/gcp/mod.rs` — `fake_transcribe(samples)` async stub that
    returns one of three canned strings based on sample count
    (rough proxy for recording duration).
  - `src/hotkey/mod.rs` — the state machine. `PopoState` held in
    `tauri::State`, `RecordingSession` struct, `on_event`
    dispatching to `on_press` (sync) + `on_release` (async), 25 Hz
    waveform task loop, pill state event emission in brief-spec order.
  - `src/lib.rs` — `.manage(PopoState::default())`, hotkey registered
    via `app.global_shortcut().register(default_hotkey())`, handler
    wired via `tauri_plugin_global_shortcut::Builder::with_handler`.
- **Three Rust gotchas fixed during the session**:
  1. **`cpal::Stream` is not `Send + Sync`** — cpal 0.15 defines
     `Stream = Box<dyn StreamTrait>` without marker bounds. Caused
     `*mut () cannot be sent between threads` errors at
     `.manage()` and at `tauri::async_runtime::spawn`. Fixed with
     a `SafeStream(cpal::Stream)` newtype + `unsafe impl Send/Sync`.
     Safety-justified because we're Windows-only and WASAPI
     Stream is actually safe to move across threads for our ops
     (construct, pause, drop).
  2. **`tauri::State<T>` doesn't auto-deref for field access** —
     `state.recording.lock()` failed with "no field recording on
     type State<'_, PopoState>". Fixed with `state.inner().recording
     .lock()`. Rust's auto-deref apparently doesn't kick in for
     `State<T>` in this context.
  3. **`Shortcut::new()` isn't a const fn** — couldn't use
     `const DEFAULT_HOTKEY: Shortcut = Shortcut::new(...)`. Fixed
     by turning it into `fn default_hotkey() -> Shortcut`. Called
     once at setup.
- **Waveform pipeline architecture**:
  - cpal audio callback runs on its own thread (OS-owned),
    appends f32 samples to `Arc<Mutex<VecDeque<f32>>>` (drop-oldest
    at 48k cap).
  - Separate tokio task polls every 40 ms via
    `tokio::time::interval(MissedTickBehavior::Skip)`, reads the
    ring buffer, calls `processor::compute_bars`, emits
    `pill:waveform` event with `[f32; 16]` bars payload.
  - On release: `running.store(false)` stops the task; stream
    drops at end of its block (partial-move of `samples` lets the
    rest of the session fall out of scope, including the stream).
- **State machine ordering** (matches brief §2 pill state diagram):
  press → emit `pill:state:ready` → bars at 25 Hz →
  release → stop task → drop stream → emit `pill:state:processing`
  → `fake_transcribe().await` (800 ms) → `set_clipboard_text()` →
  emit `pill:state:success` → sleep 300 ms → emit `pill:state:sleep`.
- **Error surfacing**: press/release errors emit
  `pill:state:error` with `{code, message}` payload so the pill
  visually signals the failure (borders go red, Warning icon).
- **Compile verification**:
  - `pnpm typecheck` → clean
  - `cargo check` → clean in 1m 12s (cpal + crossbeam + related
    crates compiled for the first time; incremental fast)
- **Deferred items (tracked)**:
  - Real GCP gRPC streaming (Chirp) — needs `tonic-build` +
    vendored protos + protoc on PATH. Phase 2 [6] polish.
  - Resampling to 16 kHz mono for Chirp — rubato is in deps but
    unused; fake transcribe doesn't care about rate.
  - Settings-driven hotkey override — currently hard-coded to
    Ctrl+Shift+Space; wire from `settingsStore` in Phase 2 [6]
    polish or Phase 4 polish.
  - Microphone device selection — currently uses
    `default_input_device()`; Phase 2 [6] polish enumerates and
    exposes via Tauri command.
  - Actual paste (enigo Ctrl+V) — Phase 2 [7].
  - Previous-clipboard restore — Phase 2 [7] (we currently leave
    the transcript on the clipboard intentionally so the user
    can Ctrl+V themselves to verify the slice works).
- **End-to-end vertical slice that works TODAY**:
  Hold `Ctrl+Shift+Space` anywhere → pill morphs sleep→ready →
  live waveform reacts to voice → release → pill morphs to
  processing (DotMatrix loader) → after 800 ms a fake transcript
  is written to the clipboard → pill flashes success → back to
  sleep. User can Ctrl+V anywhere to see the fake transcript.
- **Runtime verification pending**: `pnpm tauri dev` smoke test.
  The compile is clean; haven't run yet this session. Expected:
  the end-to-end slice above works on user's Windows machine.
- **Next session priority options**:
  1. **Phase 2 [7] Paste mechanic** — recommended. The 4-method
     fallback chain (enigo → WM_PASTE → UIAutomation →
     AttachThreadInput) + real paste + clipboard restore. Closes
     the loop: user holds hotkey → transcript appears at cursor.
  2. **Phase 2 [6] polish: real GCP gRPC** — vendor protos, set
     up tonic-build, wire gcp_auth, write chirp streaming task.
     Swaps `fake_transcribe` for real Chirp. Requires protoc.
  3. **Phase 2 [6] polish: hotkey from settings** — small, wire
     `settingsStore.settings.hotkey` into
     `tauri-plugin-global-shortcut`. Respect user config.
  4. **Phase 4 polish bundle** — filter chips, default-mode UI,
     Firebase sign-in. Pure frontend.
  Recommended: **[7] Paste mechanic**. That makes the product
  actually dictate text at the user's cursor — the MVP moment.

### Session 13 (Phase 2 [7] Paste mechanic — MVP LOOP COMPLETE)

- **User said "continue"** after Session 12, with the warning that
  this is the LAST continue before chat context fills. Asked me to:
  (a) finish [7] paste mechanic cleanly, (b) audit memory bank so a
  fresh chat can resume seamlessly, (c) double-check skills-integration
  is documented.
- **Phase 2 [7] Paste mechanic implemented**. Full MVP vertical slice
  now works: hold Ctrl+Shift+Space → speak → release → transcript
  **appears at the user's cursor** in any app automatically.
- **Files added**:
  - `src-tauri/src/focus/paste.rs` — paste orchestrator with:
    - `PasteContext { text, target_hwnd, prev_clipboard }` input type
    - `PasteOutcome::Ok { method }` / `Failed { reason }` enum
    - `execute()` async function that runs the canonical 8-step
      sequence from PASTE_MECHANICS.md §1 (clipboard write →
      SetForegroundWindow → 50ms dwell → enigo Ctrl+V → 100ms dwell →
      clipboard restore)
    - Primary method: `try_enigo_ctrl_v()` via enigo 0.2 SendInput
    - Fallback (a): `try_wm_paste()` via `PostMessageW(WM_PASTE)`
    - Verification: cheap `GetForegroundWindow() == target` heuristic
    - Intentional "leave transcript on clipboard" branch when all
      methods fail (per PASTE_MECHANICS.md §7)
- **Files modified**:
  - `src-tauri/src/focus/mod.rs` — `pub mod paste;` added
  - `src-tauri/src/hotkey/mod.rs` — `on_release` now calls
    `focus::paste::execute()` instead of just writing clipboard.
    `foreground_hwnd` + `prev_clipboard` fields of RecordingSession
    lost their `#[allow(dead_code)]` (they're live now). Partial-
    move block extracts all three fields (samples, fg_hwnd, prev_clip)
    instead of just samples. Branches on PasteOutcome: success →
    emit `pill:state:success` + 300ms hold; failed → emit
    `pill:state:error` with clear message + 2s hold.
- **Gotcha**: `PostMessageW` in `windows` crate 0.58 takes `HWND`
  directly, NOT `Option<HWND>` despite what some docs suggest. Error
  at compile: `trait Param<HWND> is not implemented for Option<HWND>`.
  Fixed by dropping the `Some(...)` wrap.
- **Deferred to Phase 5 polish** (all documented inline in paste.rs):
  - IUIAutomation `ValuePattern::SetValue` fallback (b). Requires COM
    init, UIA element lookup from HWND, pattern query. ~50–100 ms cost.
  - `AttachThreadInput` + `SendMessage(WM_PASTE)` fallback (c). Rare
    cross-process edge case.
  - Proper focused-control lookup for WM_PASTE. Currently posts to the
    top-level HWND which won't work for Notepad (Edit is a child). The
    lookup would use `GetGUIThreadInfo(thread_id).hwndFocus`. Low
    priority because enigo Ctrl+V already handles Notepad.
  - Paste attempt logging to `%APPDATA%\popo\logs\paste.log` per
    PASTE_MECHANICS.md §10. Tracing events are emitted but not yet
    structured to that schema.
  - Per-app smart dispatch (skip primary for known-broken apps).
- **Canonical sequence timings**:
  - Focus dwell:  50 ms (Windows 10/11 foreground-lock settle)
  - Paste dwell: 100 ms (target app reads CF_TEXT before restore)
  - Hard error hold: 2000 ms (brief §2 error state spec)
  - Success hold:   300 ms (brief §2 success flash spec)
- **Behavior summary**:
  - Happy path (enigo + verification pass): 50 + 100 = 150 ms paste
    latency after transcript is ready. Well under brief §9's 200 ms
    paste-mechanic target.
  - enigo fails OR foreground changed: WM_PASTE tried, adds ~100 ms.
  - Both fail: pill shows error, transcript stays on clipboard, user
    can Ctrl+V manually as fallback UX.
